# meta-deploy.list: the queue meta-deploy.bash runs, in this order.
#
# STANDING RULE (CLAUDE/AgentNotes.md): this list is exactly what needs running NOW. Add a
# run when it is pending, remove it once it has run and is done, leave out what cannot
# usefully run yet, then ask the owner to run meta-deploy.bash. Every step a run needs lives
# in the plan's own scripts, so this is the only command the owner is handed. The order is by
# dependency. A FAILED ENTRY DOES NOT STOP THE RUN: every entry runs, each one's verdict is in
# the summary, and the batch exits 1 if any failed (a plan's acceptance is skipped only when
# its own deploy failed). Only changed-plays stops inside itself, at its first failing play.
# After a run, the entries that passed are taken out and the failed ones stay for the next.
# A plan with none of triage.bash, deploy.bash and acceptance.bash has nothing
# for the script to do. A triage-only plan is run once, read-only. Words after the folder
# name are passed to that plan's triage.bash, both runs.
#
# One entry per line, four kinds:
#   <plan folder>                  its triage, deploy and acceptance scripts, in order. The
#                                  folder is named as it sits in CLAUDE/Plan/, with no
#                                  CLAUDE/Plan/ in front (00123-name, not CLAUDE/Plan/00123-name)
#   playbooks/<path>.yml [args]    a change no plan owns; run as one unit through its own
#                                  shebang, which goes through run.bash, exactly as
#                                  ./playbooks/imports/<play>.yml does by hand. Words after
#                                  it are ansible-playbook arguments: name the play's tags
#                                  (--tags lightning) so only the changed part runs. A
#                                  tagged run never decides the play's state, so a later
#                                  changed-plays still runs the play in full if it is due
#   <plan folder>/<script>.bash    one plan script, for a step none of the three fixed names
#                                  fits; run as one unit, like a playbook entry, except
#                                  that a script named *acceptance*.bash may exit 2 for
#                                  COULD NOT ESTABLISH, as an acceptance.bash may
#   changed-plays                  every play that has to run again here (one that has run
#                                  here, or one with no ledger record whose files here
#                                  show it ran), as one unit: the checkout's
#                                  ./run.bash --changed --yes, in playbook-main.yml order,
#                                  stopping at the first failure; the other plays with no
#                                  record (NEVER, UNJUDGED) are named and never run
#
# changed-plays runs WHERE IT STANDS in the list, like every other entry, and decides which
# plays it runs when it starts, so the entries above it have already run (a play an entry
# above ran is no longer changed, and is not run twice). Put it above the plan entries whose
# deploy or acceptance expects the changed plays to have run, which is the usual case, and
# below an entry it must follow. It replaces a hand-copied list of stale plays: name a
# playbooks/ entry only for a play that must run at a fixed point between two plan steps.
# Its description says which changed plays the agent expects it to pick up and why.
#
# A line starting with # is a comment and is the description of the entry below it: it is how
# the owner learns what a run will do, so write it. Blank lines are ignored. Any other line
# that is not an entry is a fatal error naming its line number.

# Issues #393, #391, #396, #395 and #394 (ccy 3.124.31, container 2.84.72). #393: ccy-teams takes a teamchat seat only for a
# session with a ccy session record. A ccy --teams (or cc --teams) launch from a pane already
# on ccy's tmux, or with no terminal, has no record, so the seat sweep released its seat about a
# minute after it started; such a launch now stops at once with exit 78, naming the starts that
# write a record (ccy or cc at a terminal outside ccy's tmux, or ccy-sessions declare then
# ccy-sessions restore). A session's retry of its own take that stopped part way (homeserver
# down) now goes on instead of finding the seat held. The image is rebuilt once for its
# teamchat. The check applies to launches after this run; a running session is unchanged.
# #391: the host tools that wait for a Claude Code session to be idle (ccy-sessions set-going
# and wind-down, ccy --stop, the team restarts) now see a turn running in the newer Claude Code
# screen layout, whose interrupt hint is in the mode line below the input box, so they no
# longer type into a running turn (ccy-sessions, ccy-teams, the claude-yolo front and
# ccy-token-usable are rebuilt). #396 (ccy 3.124.25): ccy-sessions restore takes session names
# and judges only those, so a stale record no longer fails the restore of another session; a
# stale record's message says how to remove it, with the new verb ccy-sessions forget NAME; and
# restore --dry-run prints "would fail <name>: <why>" for each record that would fail. #395:
# ccy-sessions verify-restore says, for a session still SETTING-GOING that set-going has not
# typed into, whether a set-going is running to do it or that none is.
# #394 (ccy 3.124.31, container 2.84.72): a teamchat hook input that could not be used is named
# by its hook event, reason and byte count (never its content) in link.log, session.json,
# status.json and the status line, so the next one seen after a restore says which hook it was.
# The image is rebuilt once more for its teamchat; sessions started after the rebuild show it.
# #398 (ccy 3.124.38): a session record whose file is not named after its name= (edited or
# renamed by hand) is acted on through the file it was read from: ending a session (the
# ccy-sessions picker, ccy --stop) removes it instead of leaving a stale record, a restored or
# restarted session's trampoline removes that file, and the refusals name it. ccy-sessions and
# ccy-teams are rebuilt; the image is unchanged.
# #397 (ccy 3.124.38): the claude-yolo front is rebuilt so ccy's daily Claude Code update check
# says when the image's Claude Code is newer than every screen capture, and how to capture it.
# #386 (ccy 3.124.38, container 2.84.76): ccy-teams is rebuilt with the new verb
# ccy-teams seats abandon <team> [<member>...] --reason "<text>", which releases the held seats
# of a team whose server is gone for good (purged or renamed) without a logout, recording the
# abandonment in each seat's released.json. Nothing is released by the run itself: a seat
# release is unchanged, so the dead seats stay held until the owner runs the verb from the
# checkout they were taken in (run elsewhere, it refuses, naming the pool it looked in). A
# session once launched with the team keeps it listed in its desired.json, so the verb names
# that file and --allow-known-team <team> is the expected route. The image is rebuilt for its
# copy of the seat store.
# #404 (ccy 3.124.39): ccy-teams is rebuilt so seats abandon's known-team check reads only the
# session folders under the seat store's control/: the launcher's disconnect.log there no
# longer refuses every run, so --allow-known-team is needed only where a session lists the team.
# #409 (ccy 3.124.40): ccy --teams <seats> --continue is still refused, but the refusal prints
# ccy --resume <id> --teams <seats> with the id the seat's session recorded, or, with none, the
# bare ccy --resume picker; ccy-teams is rebuilt with ccy-teams seats conversation <seats>,
# which prints that id. The image is unchanged.
# #394 fix (ccy 3.124.41, container 2.84.77): teamchat's hook reads only the fields it uses from
# Claude Code's payload, so every SessionStart is usable again: the link says started, /exit no
# longer ends 78 and marks the seat launch-failed, and session.json records the real source.
# The image is rebuilt for its teamchat; sessions started after the rebuild have the fix.
# #411 (ccy 3.124.43): ccy-sessions is rebuilt and the launcher's session-registry library deployed so
# a session launched with a bare ccy --resume (the picker), such as ccy --resume --teams <seats>,
# is no longer put in the restore manifest as resuming --teams. The image is unchanged.
# Plan 00265 R44 (ccy 3.124.49, container 2.84.80): the image is rebuilt so its teamchat kit
# carries the coordinator's standard procedure (the teamchat:coordinator skill) and a coordinator
# seat's SessionStart names it; sessions started after the rebuild see it.
# Plan 00265 R5, R55 (ccy 3.124.50): ccy-teams is rebuilt so a bare ccy/cc --teams <team> with no
# free seat of its kind adds one (one sudo) and launches it instead of exiting 78, and each
# launcher is offered and given only its own kind's seats. The image is unchanged.
# Its review fixes (ccy 3.124.51) ride the same run: a named LXC seat is refused naming its kind.
# Plan 00265 R57 (ccy 3.124.66, container 2.84.86): the image is rebuilt once so its teamchat
# version prints the image-source digest (the same one the teamchat play below gives the host's
# teamchat for the same source), and its source-commit label is named ccy-image-source-commit.
# Plan 00265 R36 (ccy 3.124.68, container 2.84.88): the same run rebuilds the image and the host's
# Go commands so files written whole (fsx) create their temporary exclusively (#417). R36
# (ccy 3.124.69): ccy-teams remove also forgets the team's seat conversation records. R36
# (ccy 3.124.70): ccy-teams' Start a ccy session adds --fresh for a seat never used here.
# Plan 00265 R82: the same run builds and installs /var/local/claude-code/chat-client-info, the
# one command a cc --teams seat may use to read the chat clients' flatpak info, permissions and
# running instances (no arguments, no other flatpak verb). The deny rules are unchanged.
# Issue #430 (ccy 3.124.87): ccy-teams and the claude-yolo front are rebuilt so a bare ccy --teams
# or cc --teams list leaves out a teamchat team whose bus does not answer (each asked at most
# 5 s, all at once), naming each above the list with why.
# Issue #431 (ccy 3.124.86, 3.124.88): ccy-sessions is rebuilt. A restore no longer starts a
# session fresh while its checkout holds a conversation recorded under another session name; it
# refuses, naming ccy-sessions retire-conversation CHECKOUT NAME, the new verb that retires that
# record so the session can start fresh on purpose. ccy-sessions forget now retires the
# forgotten session's conversation record too. Both apply to restores run after this play.
# Plan 00265 Task 4.2 (ccy 3.124.89, 3.124.91): ccy-teams and the claude-yolo front are rebuilt.
# ccy --teams <team> (and cc --teams <team>) takes the seat this checkout last worked in and asks
# only when two seats have a conversation here, in words ("last ran 2 hours ago"); a bare
# --teams with one team takes it; no message, help line or picker row shows a seat number. It
# applies to launches after this play.
# Plan 00267 Tasks 7.6, 7.7 (container 2.84.95): this play rebuilds the image, once, so its
# teamchat delivers by size only (as many messages as fit 12 KiB, no 8-message cap, the rest
# counted as pending) and in sections, one per kind of message, then teamchat's own facts.
# Sessions started after the rebuild get it; play-teamchat below does the same for the host's
# teamchat kit.
# Issue #324: the image's teamchat is built from the paths package, which gains the restore
# drill's names (container 2.84.97); the rebuild is the same single one, and nothing in the
# image reads the new names.
# Plan 00267 Tasks 2.1-2.3 (container 2.84.96, 2.84.98, 2.84.99): the same single rebuild gives
# the image's teamchat a read-receipt queue that, when full, keeps each room's latest receipt
# (none is lost); a drop ledger of the events a member drops that were addressed to it, kept a
# week, with a `bounced` receipt owed to each sender; and the sender of those owed receipts,
# which posts each as a teamchat.receipt in the team's reports room and keeps it until the
# homeserver has it. Sessions started after the rebuild get it.
# Plan 00265 R90 (ccy 3.124.94): a plain cc in ccy's tmux, in a checkout holding a host seat of
# one teamchat team, joins that team (cc --no-teams starts on none); a launch with nobody to
# ask takes the seat this checkout last worked in, and says so.
# Plan 00265 R89, R90 (ccy 3.124.97): ccy-sessions and ccy-teams are rebuilt. ccy-sessions
# restore (no names) now also starts team seats: each teamchat seat with a conversation recorded
# in its seat checkout that no running session holds and no record covers, each said in words
# ("would start the coordinator of <team> in <checkout>, resuming ..."). A new team session is
# named by its seat's role when no other seat of the team there has it
# (ccy-<team>-<project>-coordinator); the number stays only to tell two workers apart. Running
# sessions keep their names.
# Plan 00265 batch 26 fixes (ccy 3.124.99): ccy-teams reads a team's status record under its
# configured teamchat root (on this host the same path as before, so nothing changes here).
# Check afterwards: `ccy-sessions restore --dry-run` names the seats it would start in words,
# and a new seat session's tmux name ends in its role.
# Plan 00266 C.3 (ccy 3.124.100, container 2.84.100): ccy-sessions is rebuilt so verify-restore also reports the
# LXC containers the boot unit brings back (lxc:<container> RUNNING / NOT-RUNNING <state> /
# NOT-RECORDED, lxc NO-RECORD), wherever teamchat-lxc-boot.service is enabled, and fails on a
# recorded container not running. With the unit disabled it only says so. The same single
# image rebuild covers it (built from internal/systemd; nothing in it changes). It needs
# play-teamchat below for its sudoers rule.
# Issue #443 (ccy 3.124.101, container 2.84.101): the same single image rebuild gives the image's
# teamchat a proof that keeps what a round found when its store write fails, so the next round
# proves it (it no longer goes missing); a failed recv ack keeps its messages' content; a failed
# Stop write is recorded at the next Stop. Sessions started after the rebuild get it.
# Plan 00267 Task 3.1 (ccy 3.124.102, container 2.84.102): the same single rebuild gives the
# image's teamchat rate and budget holds instead of drops. An agent's message past either limit
# waits for the window's next free slot, then is delivered. Past pending's agent share it is
# bounced `rate`, with a receipt to its sender. Sessions started after the rebuild get it.
# Container 2.84.103 (ccy 3.124.104) is the same single rebuild: only teamchat's tests changed.
playbooks/imports/play-claude-yolo.yml

# Plan 00265 R23 (issue #425): teamchat run --kind lxc --key now reads the key, so a v2 LXC
# member's session can start at all (it was refused 78 "a lxc launch needs --desired", on the
# homeserver's own host too). This run rebuilds and deploys the teamchat member kit on this host
# and copies it into the declared same-host LXC member containers; play-claude-yolo above
# rebuilds the ccy image for its teamchat. Nothing running is restarted: an LXC member session
# started after the run (teamchat-admin lxc start --member <container>/<key>) starts, instead
# of exiting 78. R44: the host kit it assembles carries the coordinator skill too, and the kit
# check now requires it. R57: the member program's digest is now the image-source digest, so the
# kit is assembled in a new digest directory and its current link moves once (the present teams
# are re-applied for the new member program, as on any member change); teamchat-admin keeps the
# src-go tree id. Plan 00266 C.2: it also installs teamchat-lxc-record.service,
# teamchat-lxc-boot.service and a drop-in /etc/systemd/system/lxc@.service.d/teamchat.conf, and,
# while an LXC member is declared present, first checks lxc@.service is installed (the run fails
# if not), records every LXC seat whose session runs now as one to restart (lxc record-started:
# ADDED or KEPT lines; nothing is started), starts the record unit and enables the boot unit
# (nothing else starts or stops now). At the next shutdown the running containers are recorded;
# at the next boot those are started as lxc@<container>.service, then their recorded seats, and a
# container that will not start no longer keeps the others' seats down (lxc boot).
# Plan 00267 Tasks 7.6, 7.7: the host kit and the LXC members' copies carry the same teamchat as
# the image above (deliveries limited by size only, in sections); member sessions started after
# the run get it.
# Issue #324 (the restore drill): teamchat-admin's restore now reads a restore proof record and
# stays refused without one, and it gains `restore-drill`. The play gains
# teamchat_restore_drill (default false) and, only where it is true, the drill port's SELinux
# label. This host does not set it, so no drill port is labelled, no port is opened and NO
# drill runs here. Expected: changed for the teamchat-admin copy and its link; the SELinux tasks
# unchanged. Afterwards: `sudo teamchat-admin restore --team T --backup N` exits 70 naming the
# missing restore proof, as it refused before. With teamchat_restore_drill false the drill
# port's label task is skipped here (no teamchat_restore_drill_port is set); on a host that sets
# the port and turns the drill off, the label is now removed.
# Plan 00267 Phase 2 (Tasks 2.1-2.3): the host kit and the LXC members' copies carry the same
# teamchat as the image above: a full read-receipt queue keeps each room's latest receipt; a
# member keeps a drop ledger (a week) of the events addressed to it that it drops and owes each
# sender a `bounced` receipt; the link posts those receipts as teamchat.receipt in the team's
# reports room, keeping each until the homeserver has it. Member sessions started after the
# run get it.
# Plan 00268 Task 1.3: the teamchat-admin it installs gains the read-only verb
# `team traffic --team T --since <time> --until <time> [--json]`, a team's event counts per hour
# by room, type, typed key, relation and membership, with no content. Nothing runs it during
# the play; the bus host's admin seat runs it afterwards (00268's bus-host-commands.md).
# Plan 00266 C.3: teamchat-admin gains the read-only `lxc check-running`. While an LXC member
# is declared present, the play installs /etc/sudoers.d/teamchat-lxc-check-running (checked
# with visudo), which lets the teamchat owner group run that one command with no password;
# with no LXC member the file is removed. Afterwards, on such a host:
# `sudo -n /usr/local/sbin/teamchat-admin lxc check-running` prints one UP/DOWN/UNRECORDED line
# per container (NO-RECORD until the first shutdown after C.2), and
# `ccy-sessions verify-restore` ends with the lxc: lines.
# Issue #443: the host kit and the LXC members' copies carry the same teamchat as the image
# above (a refused proof, ack or Stop write loses nothing). Member sessions started after the
# run get it.
# Plan 00267 Task 3.1: the host kit and the LXC copies carry the same teamchat as the image
# above (rate and budget hold a message instead of dropping it). Member sessions started after
# the run get it.
playbooks/imports/play-teamchat.yml

# Plan 00265 R79: finishes the private-name guard's setup. It ASKS at the terminal for each
# private name that is not a team name (a client or project name), one at a time with nothing
# shown as you type, until an empty line; a name already listed is skipped. Each is vault-encrypted into
# private_name_terms in host_vars (nothing else there changes), and the names in the pinned list
# you wrote by hand (<git dir>/private-terms-pinned) are imported the same way; the list is
# decrypted again to check it, and only then is the hand-written file moved aside. It then runs
# play-git-hooks-security.yml (one sudo, to list /var/lib/teamchat-ctl), which writes the pinned
# list again (mode 0600) from host_vars' team, member and container names, the teams this bus
# host serves and private_name_terms, and prints how many names are pinned, never the names.
# The moved-aside file is deleted once the new list is written, or put back if the play fails.
# A second run is safe: press Enter at the first question to add nothing.
00265-teamchat-v2-smoke-test-register/r79-private-names.bash

# Plan 00268 Tasks 1.4, 1.7 (Lightning memory leak): rebuilds agent-bus-lightning and rewrites
# the Lightning launchers. A lightning team whose agent_bus_element_teams entry has
# lightning_diagnostics: true (set it first, in host_vars, for the busy team) is launched with a
# memory line every 5 s and a line for each event slower than 100 ms, mirrored to
# <team>/cache/lightning-diagnostic.log in its instance; it takes effect at that instance's next
# launch (close it and open it again). The run also places and, while some team has the key
# true, enables and starts the user service agent-bus-lightning-watch, which records the
# instance's threads once, beside that log, if the log falls silent for 60 s while memory rises
# 256 MiB; with no team on it is stopped and disabled. While a team is traced it installs gdb
# and elfutils (one sudo) for that record's backtrace, which kernel.yama.ptrace_scope=1 (set
# by the self-update play) refuses; the record then says so. Before setting the key, check the
# instance runs Lightning 0.10.7 or later (laptop-commands.md step 1): only 0.10.7 caps the log.
# Task 1.9: it also caps every Lightning instance's memory at 5G with no swap
# (agent_bus_lightning_memory_max in host_vars; false removes it), as a user systemd drop-in on
# the app's Flatpak scopes, and places agent-bus-lightning-capped@.service, which a kill at the
# cap starts to raise a critical desktop notification; nothing reopens Lightning. The user
# manager is reloaded; the cap holds for instances opened after the run (close and reopen each).
playbooks/imports/optional/common/play-agent-bus-element.yml --tags agent-bus-lightning

# Plan 00257 B.12 proof. Its checks are read-only: in each declared LXC member container that
# is running, the session units are present, not enabled and not linked in
# multi-user.target.wants. A stopped container is reported offline and skipped. It then ASKS,
# per running container, whether to reboot it (default no; asked at the terminal even under
# meta-deploy) and reboots only on the owner's yes, then checks no claude runs for its members'
# users. Exit 2 means it could not establish the answer: nothing judged (every container
# offline, say), fewer units found than members declared, a probe that did not answer, or a
# failed lxc-stop or lxc-start.
00257-cross-host-lxc-team-members/lxc-no-autostart-acceptance.bash

# Issue #325: play-photography's RapidRAW check now asks rpm for the package's real name,
# rapid-raw (it asked for rapidraw, so every run downloaded the RPM, found it installed and
# deleted it again), and fails on any rpm answer other than installed or not installed. The
# play runs twice, in full, as the idempotence proof: the first run may converge the host;
# the SECOND RUN MUST REPORT changed=0 in its PLAY RECAP. Any changed task there, or a
# "Download RapidRAW RPM" that is not skipped, means #325 is not fixed.
playbooks/imports/optional/common/play-photography.yml
# The second pass of the #325 proof above: its PLAY RECAP must say changed=0.
playbooks/imports/optional/common/play-photography.yml

# Issue #322 (ccy 3.124.18): line-based listings are captured first instead of read through a
# process substitution and its wait "$!". Besides the launcher, cc and agent-bus-install (whose
# plays have run), the change reaches the user tools imgpaste, qnap-finder, raw-prune,
# ssh-agent-start, ssh-keys-rekey and ssh-with-agent.bash, fedora-desktop-self-update,
# docker-in-lxc and the host-health kernel-change script. changed-plays is expected to pick up
# the plays that deploy those.
# Plan 00265 R72 and Plan 00266 C.2's review: it is also expected to pick up
# play-basic-configs.yml for the Ctrl+R history includes. A HISTFILE you set yourself (e.g.
# /dev/null) is no longer pointed back at the shared file at each prompt, and the shared-history
# redirect now lives in history-record.bash. It takes effect in terminals opened after the run.
changed-plays

# Plan 00266 Phase A (ccy 3.124.103): play-claude-yolo.yml (listed above) installs the launcher's
# libraries and rebuilds ccy-teams with `approve`. Then, at a terminal, before the next launch of
# each checkout with a .claude/ccy/mounts file: `ccy-teams approve mounts <checkout>` (read what it
# shows first). Before the next reboot, for each restored session with compose files:
# `ccy-teams approve compose <checkout>`. A refused restore is named by `ccy-sessions verify-restore`.
